<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amyntas III of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 370 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amyntas III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from about 393 to 370 BCE and father of Philip II. His reign was beset by Illyrian invasions and rival claimants, and the Chalcidian League led by Olynthus seized much of his kingdom, including Pella, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> intervened and broke the league in 379 BCE. He held his kingdom together through alliances with Sparta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amyntas-iii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Amyntas III of Macedon" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matia.gr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Amyntas_III-161113.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amyntas III belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a> through his father Arrhidaeus, a son of one of the sons of Alexander I. The death of Alexander I in 454 BCE had set his five sons against one another in a dynastic crisis, and the king's descendants later met violent ends. After Archelaus died in 400 or 399 BCE, five kings ruled in less than seven years, nearly all of them ending violently. Amyntas seized the throne in 393 or 392 BCE after assassinating the previous king, Pausanias, according to Diodorus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shortly after becoming king Amyntas was driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, but the following year he recovered his kingdom with the aid of the Thessalians. Medius, head of the house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuadae">Aleuadae</a> of Larissa, is believed to have helped him. To guard against the Illyrians he allied himself with the Chalcidian League led by Olynthus and granted it rights to Macedonian timber, which was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to help fortify its fleet. The money that flowed into Olynthus increased its power, so Amyntas sought other allies. Around 386 BCE he adopted the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iphicrates">Iphicrates</a>, who was married to a daughter of the Odrysian chief Kotys, in recognition of his military services.</p><p>When Olynthus grew to threaten him, Amyntas appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, which was anxious to re-establish itself in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387 BCE and was glad to weaken a city backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Thebes. The Spartans fought alongside him, suffering two defeats at first, and in 379 BCE destroyed Olynthus. Amyntas also entered a league with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason-of-pherae">Jason of Pherae</a> and cultivated the friendship of Athens, and in 371 BCE he voted with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to help Athens recover Amphipolis. With Olynthus gone, he could keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> revenues for himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amyntas took two wives, Eurydice and Gygaea, as Macedonian kings used polygamy to secure alliances and provide enough heirs. Eurydice bore him three sons who all became king in turn, Alexander II, Perdiccas III and Philip II, and a daughter, Eurynoe. Gygaea's sons Archelaus, Arrhidaeus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> never ruled and were all killed by their half-brother Philip II.</p><p>Amyntas died aged 50 and left his throne to his eldest son, Alexander II. Friends of Ptolemy killed Alexander at a festival in 368 BCE. Perdiccas killed Ptolemy in 365 BCE and was himself killed in battle by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> in 359 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amyntas III of Macedon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 4th century BCE the league grew aggressively, absorbing Potidaea and even occupying Pella, the Macedonian capital, during the troubles of <b>Amyntas III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalcidian-league">Chalcidian League</a></li></ul></section>
